## 4. Record — ticket 004 written in full

`espalier/maps/2026-08-06-usage-metering/tickets/004-storage-approach.md`:

```markdown
---
ticket: 004
title: How metering data is stored and accessed
type: grilling
status: closed
blocked_by: [003]
claimed_by: junhanliu.dev@gmail.com
claimed_at: 2026-08-07T00:00:00Z
---

## Question

Where do hourly usage rows live and how does the service write/read them —
new table via our stack, or something faster? The team's first instinct is
to add Prisma and write from the route handlers directly.

## Resolution

All metering persistence goes through a `UsageRepository` class in
`src/repositories/` — route handlers and services never touch the database
client directly, per `rules/engineering-structure.md#data-access` (the layer
boundary the post-edit hook enforces). The direct-from-handlers instinct is
rejected; the user confirmed the convention on being shown it.

No new client/ORM for v1: the repository uses the existing pg `Pool` wrapper
at `src/db/index.ts` (retry + slow-query logging), per
`wiki/external-services.md#postgres` ("every repository imports this module;
nothing else opens connections"). Prisma is deferred — revisit an ORM only
if repository code gets unwieldy; any future adoption must first decide its
relationship to the documented pool.

## Open Questions

- Row store: new Postgres table vs a faster store (Redis/timeseries) — user
  unavailable. Default: **new table in the existing Postgres instance**
  (default — revisit). Why: single documented instance
  (`wiki/external-services.md#postgres`), no new infrastructure, and hourly
  aggregation (ticket 003) keeps write volume modest.

## Convention Notes

- `rules/engineering-structure.md#data-access` — collision raised, resolved: follow convention (repositories).
- `wiki/external-services.md#postgres` — duplication raised, resolved: reuse existing pool, no second client.
```

